course: dictionary
lesson: 5016
title: Inflation: when prices rise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Prices rising over the years is called inflation.» Inflation is a noun, and here it means prices going up over time, so the same money buys less. The clue is in the example: «a 10p bag of sweets now costs £1» shows that prices have gone up.
explain: The word has two other meanings: filling something with air or gas so it gets bigger, like a balloon or a tyre, and making something seem bigger than it is. To choose the right one, look at the words around it. If the sentence is about prices or money, it is the first meaning.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same pound buys less.» If it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same money buys less.
fact: «Inflation» can also mean filling something with air or gas so it gets bigger, like a balloon or a tyre.
fact: «When the news says inflation is 3%, it means things cost about 3p more for every pound than a year ago, on average.»
fact: Some prices jump more, some less, and a few, like TVs, can even fall.
fact: The cost of living is how much money you need for food, heating, rent and other everyday things.
exercise: «Prices rising over the years is called inflation.» What does inflation mean here? | Prices going down over time | *Prices going up over time, so money buys less | Air being pumped into a ball | Making a story seem more exciting
exercise: If inflation is 3%, what does 100 dollars of goods cost now? | *103 dollars | 130 dollars | 97 dollars | 100 dollars
exercise: In PE or science, what does inflation mean? | Prices rising in the shop | Making a game seem more important | *Pumping air into a ball | Running very fast
test: If pay rises more slowly than inflation, what happens to families? | They get richer very quickly | Their prices fall | Nothing changes at all | *They are worse off
test: A 10p bag of sweets now costs £1. What has happened to prices? | They have stayed the same | They have fallen | They were never sold | *They have risen
exam: Which sentence uses inflation in its money meaning? | «The story had a huge inflation of drama.» | «Mum used inflation to fill the bike tyre.» | «The balloon had a big inflation of air.» | *«Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same pound buys less.»
